 US refinery gasoline output ramps to 8.5 mb/d in Q4 on 16.9 mb/d runs. 2027 repeats the pattern: +22 / -471 / -285 / +147 - the summer import call is bigger next year (Q2'27 demand 10.65 mb/d, supply only 10.18). Mexico stays ~-420 to -480 kb/d short every quarter, Canada's surplus fades to +11 in Q3'27.", "Europe: surplus 827 kb/d in Q3'26 (unchanged vs 831), 864 in Q4'26 (-27 revision, supply -57 on lower runs), then 965 in Q1'27 before thinning to 771-782 kb/d through Q2-Q3'27 - the thinnest quarterly surplus in the series as demand climbs to a record 2.63 mb/d in Q3'27. Italy (+118-150) and Other Europe (+620-730) carry the length; France (-40 to -66) and Turkey (-24 to -42) are the region's shorts. Still the marginal barrel for PADD 1 and West Africa.", "Middle East: Q3'26 deficit -322 kb/d (revised -79, supply -38) and Q4'26 -239 (revised -150, supply -106 on delayed restarts), so Gulf imports of gasoline stay heavy through year-end. From Q1'27 the region moves back to balance (-18 / +52 / -11 / -63) as Saudi supply returns to ~+146-176 kb/d surplus and 'Other ME' shorts halve to ~-120-210 kb/d. Regional runs 8.1 (Q3'26) -> 10.2 mb/d (Q4'27).", "Asia Pacific is the biggest change: Q3'26 revised from -251 to -33 kb/d (supply +156) and Q4'26 from -51 to +120, then a persistent +370-440 kb/d surplus through 2027. Driver is China - demand cut ~-100 kb/d per quarter (3.2-3.3 mb/d vs 3.3-3.4 before) while supply holds, so China's balance goes from ~+30 in Q2-Q3'26 to +390-470 kb/d in 2027. India (+250-310) and Korea (+260-350) remain steady exporters; Japan flat; Australia -140 to -175.", "FSU: Q3'26 deficit widened sharply to -217 kb/d (from -35; supply -103 kb/d on refinery outages, runs 4.9 mb/d vs 6.2 in Q1) and Q4'26 to -33. Russia returns to a small surplus only in Q1'27 (+81) as runs recover to 6.3 mb/d, so Russian gasoline exports stay minimal into year-end.", "Africa: structural deficit -706 to -724 kb/d through 2026, widening to -780 to -823 in 2027 as demand grows ~70 kb/d y/y to ~1.46-1.49 mb/d while supply stalls at ~670 kb/d (Nigerian ramp plateaus). Africa remains the largest single importing region and the key outlet for European length.", "Latin America: deficit -428 kb/d in Q3'26 (80 kb/d less short than June pack) and -520 in Q4'26, rising to -511/-583 kb/d in Q3-Q4'27. Brazil, Argentina and Chile all short; Venezuelan supply small. Combined with Mexico (-440), the western hemisphere south of the US needs ~0.9-1.0 mb/d of imports - the natural home for US Gulf Coast and PADD 3 surplus (+2.85-3.16 mb/d).", "Refining margins (EA path): USGC diesel-oriented margin peaks at $34.5/bbl in Q3'26 and gasoline-oriented $29.2, then halves by Q1'27 ($18.3 / $13.3) and drops to $6.4 / $3.1 by Q4'27. NWE follows (Q3'26 $29.5 -> Q4'27 $7.2); Mediterranean and Singapore margins go negative by end-2027. Turnarounds: 6.4 mb/d offline in Sep-26 (Asia 1.5, FSU 1.8, Europe 0.8), falling to 3.8 mb/d in Oct and 1.3 mb/d by Feb-27.", "Naphtha (kt): Europe is only a net importer in Q1 (-1,060 kt Q1'26, -1,029 kt Q1'27) and Q2, turning to a surplus of +535 / +574 kt in Q4 as FSU exports run 3.4-4.3 Mt per quarter; Germany (-600 to -1,250 kt) and the Netherlands (-580 to -1,120 kt) are the big shorts, Italy and Other Europe the exporters. Asia-Pacific deficit widens from -8.3 to -9.2 Mt/qtr in Q2-Q4'26 to -12.7 to -14.5 Mt in 2027, almost entirely China (-3.1 Mt Q3'26 -> -8.9 Mt Q4'27) as Korea's short shrinks - the Asian pull on Mediterranean/US/Algerian naphtha strengthens next year. US stays ~5-6 Mt/qtr long."], "conclusion": "Net read: the acute Q2-Q3'26 gasoline squeeze is being resolved by Asian supply (China demand weaker, runs back) rather than by demand destruction in the West - the global deficit shrinks from -1.2 mb/d to roughly balanced by Q1'27, then sits at -0.1 to -0.5 mb/d through 2027. That supports an easing of Atlantic Basin cracks into 2027, but with Europe's surplus thinning to ~0.77-0.78 mb/d by Q2-Q3'27 against Africa (-0.8), Latin America (-0.5) and Mexico (-0.45) all growing, the Atlantic Basin itself stays structurally tight in the summer; the length is in the Pacific (Asia-Pacific +0.4 mb/d), which matters mainly for arbitrage direction (East-to-West flows) rather than outright price."}}